1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:03,360
Once a screen position is known,

2
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:07,060
why does stored depth differ from distance,
and why do we still need w?

3
00:00:07,420 --> 00:00:09,640
First, compare forward distance with stored
depth,

4
00:00:09,640 --> 00:00:13,480
then observe overlapping buildings in an
actual game.

5
00:00:13,840 --> 00:00:15,780
Then use slanted roof patterns to explain

6
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:19,420
perspective-correct interpolation of texture
values.

7
00:00:19,820 --> 00:00:22,100
Finally, solve three viewport exercises and
organize

8
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:25,620
how to locate the first incorrect coordinate
stage.

9
00:00:26,383 --> 00:00:30,223
Retain depth after mappingx andy to pixels;

10
00:00:30,463 --> 00:00:34,103
it helps determine the front surface where
triangles overlap.

11
00:00:34,483 --> 00:00:39,103
Our perspective depth is1.1 minus1.1/z,

12
00:00:39,403 --> 00:00:41,743
so it is not linear in forward distance.

13
00:00:41,863 --> 00:00:45,223
Atz2 depth is0.55; atz4 it is0.825.

14
00:00:45,254 --> 00:00:49,623
Equal distance increments do not produce equal
stored-depth increments.

15
00:00:49,703 --> 00:00:55,943
Atz6 depth is about0.9167, not0.5 even though6
is midway between1 and11.

16
00:00:55,943 --> 00:00:59,543
Orthographic depth uses(z−1)/10;z6 maps

17
00:00:59,943 --> 00:01:02,943
to0.5 and varies linearly with distance.

18
00:01:03,183 --> 00:01:07,243
Precision depends on the projection mapping
and storage format together;

19
00:01:07,356 --> 00:01:11,643
floating-point storage alone does not
guarantee equal distance precision.

20
00:01:12,103 --> 00:01:16,483
The source’sw-buffering is historical
distance-related storage;

21
00:01:16,843 --> 00:01:21,063
constant orthographicw1 cannot distinguish
depths by itself.

22
00:01:21,717 --> 00:01:24,757
Watch the front platform and taller rear
buildings as new

23
00:01:24,997 --> 00:01:28,717
small structures add walls in front of the
long railing.

24
00:01:31,411 --> 00:01:35,191
Surfaces at similar screen positions may cover
one another;

25
00:01:35,491 --> 00:01:39,611
screenx andy alone cannot determine that
relationship.

26
00:01:42,345 --> 00:01:44,745
Elevated views expose interior platforms,

27
00:01:44,745 --> 00:01:50,905
while lower views overlap roofs and walls;
notice which surface comes first.

28
00:01:53,740 --> 00:01:58,340
Computed depth is the output of a chosen
mapping,

29
00:01:58,340 --> 00:02:02,500
not a distance label;0.825 is not0.825 world
units.

30
00:02:05,234 --> 00:02:09,074
State which direction represents nearer and
farther surfaces. In this example the

31
00:02:09,374 --> 00:02:14,274
stored depth at the near plane is zero, and
the stored depth at the far plane is one.

32
00:02:14,454 --> 00:02:21,088
We have not established the game’s depth
representation;

33
00:02:21,408 --> 00:02:24,668
this footage motivates the need for occlusion
handling.

34
00:02:27,302 --> 00:02:32,402
Compare depth values produced by the same
camera and projection;

35
00:02:32,402 --> 00:02:35,682
values from different views are not directly
comparable.

36
00:02:38,477 --> 00:02:42,137
Retaining intermediate coordinates lets you
check world

37
00:02:42,137 --> 00:02:45,857
placement,view boundaries and depth comparison
in turn.

38
00:02:46,717 --> 00:02:50,037
The role ofw continues into
perspective-correct interpolation

39
00:02:50,277 --> 00:02:54,957
of texture coordinates and other vertex
attributes.

40
00:02:55,297 --> 00:02:59,957
For a separate edge example,

41
00:02:59,957 --> 00:03:04,717
endpoints havew1 and4 and texture coordinateu0
and1.

42
00:03:04,977 --> 00:03:09,357
A direct screen-space average gives0.5,

43
00:03:09,557 --> 00:03:13,857
but a projected midpoint is not the geometric
midpoint of the3D edge.

44
00:03:13,917 --> 00:03:22,017
Interpolateu/w and1/w separately, then divide
the interpolated results.

45
00:03:22,437 --> 00:03:28,657
Add0 and0.25 and divide by2 to get0.125. This
numerator equals1/8.

46
00:03:28,657 --> 00:03:36,477
For the denominator, add1 and0.25 and divide
by2. The result is0.625,which equals5/8.

47
00:03:36,757 --> 00:03:40,097
The correctu is0.2, rather than0.5,

48
00:03:40,097 --> 00:03:45,257
explaining how a texture remains attached
under perspective.

49
00:03:45,637 --> 00:03:50,797
For triangles, use three screen-space weights
with the same principle;

50
00:03:51,077 --> 00:03:55,537
hardware commonly handles this unless another
interpolation mode is requested.

51
00:03:56,283 --> 00:04:00,663
Using a plane atz1 is convenient; moving the
virtual plane

52
00:04:00,983 --> 00:04:05,903
to2 can preserve the final image if the view
is kept unchanged.

53
00:04:06,163 --> 00:04:09,143
Ray intersections double,

54
00:04:09,503 --> 00:04:13,783
but so do the rectangle’s width and height on
that plane.

55
00:04:14,083 --> 00:04:16,543
The point’s fraction of the rectangle stays
unchanged,

56
00:04:16,643 --> 00:04:19,743
yielding the same final pixel position.

57
00:04:19,963 --> 00:04:25,123
A physical camera with a fixed sensor and
changing focal length

58
00:04:25,123 --> 00:04:28,463
has different conditions, as the preceding
lesson illustrated.

59
00:04:28,843 --> 00:04:32,643
Multiplying all four clip components by the
same positive factor

60
00:04:33,463 --> 00:04:37,523
preserves their divided position; doubling
also doublesw to8.

61
00:04:37,983 --> 00:04:42,083
Still verify the pre-divisionw value and
supported hardware conditions;

62
00:04:42,503 --> 00:04:46,743
equal images do not imply every internal
operation is identical.

63
00:04:47,533 --> 00:04:51,293
Choose a roof pattern and nearby window;

64
00:04:55,500 --> 00:05:02,507
the pattern remains on the building surface as
the view changes.

65
00:05:02,827 --> 00:05:09,307
Screen spacing alone cannot determine matching
surface fractions across a tilted face.

66
00:05:13,413 --> 00:05:18,233
Our edge calculation explained why a screen
midpoint needsu0.2;

67
00:05:18,533 --> 00:05:29,227
that number was not measured from this game.

68
00:05:29,407 --> 00:05:33,347
We do not infer the shader that creates this
roof pattern;

69
00:05:37,593 --> 00:05:40,733
it motivates the required connection between
surface and image.

70
00:05:40,733 --> 00:05:50,260
The texture lesson continues this connection
by assigningUV coordinates to surfaces.

71
00:05:51,200 --> 00:05:55,780
In a conventional graphics path, the vertex
shader outputs clip coordinates,

72
00:05:56,060 --> 00:05:59,640
rather than pre-divided NDC or screen pixels.

73
00:05:59,920 --> 00:06:05,340
The pipeline then clips triangles,divides and
maps to the viewport;

74
00:06:05,700 --> 00:06:10,040
our manual calculation explains those
automatic stages.

75
00:06:10,300 --> 00:06:16,560
CPU calculations can place name labels or test
visibility;

76
00:06:16,640 --> 00:06:19,720
retainw and the clipping conditions there too.

77
00:06:19,720 --> 00:06:23,380
One point inside the view does not prove an
entire object visible;

78
00:06:23,760 --> 00:06:28,160
bounds,occlusion andLOD selection serve
different purposes.

79
00:06:28,600 --> 00:06:31,860
Lighting must combine geometry and lights in
the same space; world or view space

80
00:06:32,220 --> 00:06:37,900
can be chosen, but values from different
spaces cannot be directly subtracted.

81
00:06:38,280 --> 00:06:41,860
A light can have a camera-like viewing space,

82
00:06:42,320 --> 00:06:45,540
useful for shadow maps and projectedgobo
images.

83
00:06:45,620 --> 00:06:50,540
A surface can carry tangent,bitangent and
normal axes;

84
00:06:50,940 --> 00:06:55,860
their construction returns in theUV and
normal-mapping lessons.

85
00:06:56,683 --> 00:07:00,243
For practice, keep the local point and model
translation but move the camera to(3,1,1),

86
00:07:00,243 --> 00:07:05,943
with rotation and projection unchanged.

87
00:07:06,025 --> 00:07:10,923
Subtracting the new camera gives view(1,1,4),

88
00:07:10,923 --> 00:07:14,663
changing only the horizontal component.

89
00:07:14,863 --> 00:07:22,923
Clip becomes(1,16/9,3.3,4); dividedx is0.25
whiley and depth stay unchanged.

90
00:07:22,923 --> 00:07:27,003
The same viewport yields(600,175);

91
00:07:27,523 --> 00:07:31,683
moving the camera right moves this fixed point
left in the image.

92
00:07:32,223 --> 00:07:35,743
For another exercise, change only the viewport
origin to(0,0);

93
00:07:36,603 --> 00:07:40,943
the original point’s NDC stays fixed and
screen position becomes(600,125).

94
00:07:41,343 --> 00:07:45,943
Using the full1920-by1080 viewport instead
places the original point at(1440,300);

95
00:07:45,943 --> 00:07:49,443
distinguish it from the offset small-window
result.

96
00:07:49,723 --> 00:07:53,883
Mark only the changed input and find the first
differing stage

97
00:07:53,883 --> 00:07:57,743
among local,world,view,clip and screen to
narrow down errors.

98
00:07:58,650 --> 00:08:02,650
Look at the arch between buildings and the
front terrace;

99
00:08:02,890 --> 00:08:06,910
keep finding the same landmark through wider
and rotating views.

100
00:08:09,037 --> 00:08:12,877
When it disappears, first check foreground
occlusion and avoid

101
00:08:13,137 --> 00:08:18,643
confusing a similar arch elsewhere with the
original point.

102
00:08:18,883 --> 00:08:23,803
Adding blocks changes terrace height and
structure;

103
00:08:24,183 --> 00:08:27,003
distinguish those moments from camera-only
movement.

104
00:08:27,003 --> 00:08:32,590
Railings and small trees near a high terrace

105
00:08:32,790 --> 00:08:35,570
help identify which surface is being viewed.

106
00:08:37,777 --> 00:08:43,617
In a real development project, name an
object-attached point and

107
00:08:43,937 --> 00:08:46,717
log its local coordinates,then world and view
values in order.

108
00:08:49,003 --> 00:08:51,723
Log all four clip components before division;

109
00:08:52,183 --> 00:08:56,663
omittingw obscures the connection between
clipping and the projected result.

110
00:08:58,730 --> 00:09:02,610
Record viewport origin and size when they
change;

111
00:09:02,630 --> 00:09:06,910
mapping a split window with full-screen
dimensions misplaces the marker.

112
00:09:08,957 --> 00:09:12,777
These game pixels do not reveal the
intermediate numbers;

113
00:09:13,117 --> 00:09:16,797
practice with actual values accessible in your
own project.

114
00:09:18,883 --> 00:09:24,763
Depth and surface patterns show that the chain
also supports

115
00:09:25,023 --> 00:09:27,703
occlusion and interpolation, beyond moving a
single point.

116
00:09:27,943 --> 00:09:34,290
When the result is wrong, inspect the first
incorrect transform;

117
00:09:34,450 --> 00:09:37,690
carry that method into the upcoming mesh and
texture exercises.

118
00:09:38,650 --> 00:09:44,850
Our point moved through
local(1,0,0),world(4,2,5)

119
00:09:44,850 --> 00:09:49,930
andview(2,1,4): the same point expressed in
different frames.

120
00:09:50,310 --> 00:09:53,750
The projection prepared zoom and depth withw4;

121
00:09:54,170 --> 00:09:57,450
clipping preceded division into normalized
coordinates.

122
00:09:58,030 --> 00:10:02,810
The800-by450 viewport at(100,50) placed the
point at(700,175);

123
00:10:02,810 --> 00:10:06,150
viewport placement is part of the calculation.

124
00:10:06,190 --> 00:10:11,970
Depth is not metric distance,

125
00:10:12,250 --> 00:10:14,550
and interpolation still usesw information
after coordinate division.

126
00:10:14,850 --> 00:10:19,250
Next we connect vertices into meshes and
attachUV coordinates;

127
00:10:19,610 --> 00:10:22,790
today’s transforms move those surfaces onto
the screen.
